============================================================
1. WHAT IS A LOOP?
============================================================

A loop repeatedly executes a block of code while a condition
is satisfied.

Example:

    Print "Hello" 5 times.

Without loop:

    printf("Hello");
    printf("Hello");
    printf("Hello");
    printf("Hello");
    printf("Hello");

With loop:

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        printf("Hello");
    }


General idea:

              START
                |
                v
            CONDITION
             /      \
          TRUE      FALSE
           |          |
           v          v
        BODY        EXIT
           |
           |
           +--------> CONDITION


============================================================
2. WHY DO WE NEED LOOPS?
============================================================

Suppose you want to print numbers from 1 to 1000.

Without loop:

    printf("1");
    printf("2");
    ...
    printf("1000");


This is:

    repetitive
    difficult to maintain
    error-prone
    inefficient to write


Using loop:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 1000; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }


One loop replaces hundreds or thousands of statements.


============================================================
3. TYPES OF LOOPS IN C
============================================================

C provides three primary loop statements:

    1. while
    2. do-while
    3. for


Conceptually:

    while
       ↓
    condition checked BEFORE body


    do-while
       ↓
    body executes BEFORE condition is checked


    for
       ↓
    compact form commonly used for counter-controlled loops


============================================================
4. WHILE LOOP
============================================================

Syntax:

    while (condition)
    {
        statements;
    }


Execution:

    1. Evaluate condition.
    2. If true, execute body.
    3. Return to condition.
    4. Repeat.
    5. If false, exit loop.


Flow:

          condition
          /      \
       true      false
        |          |
       body       exit
        |
        +---------> condition


============================================================
5. SIMPLE while EXAMPLE
============================================================

    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 5)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }


Output:

    1 2 3 4 5


Step-by-step:

    i = 1
    1 <= 5 → true → print 1
    i = 2

    2 <= 5 → true → print 2
    i = 3

    3 <= 5 → true → print 3
    i = 4

    4 <= 5 → true → print 4
    i = 5

    5 <= 5 → true → print 5
    i = 6

    6 <= 5 → false → stop


============================================================
6. IMPORTANT PARTS OF while LOOP
============================================================

A typical while loop has:

    Initialization
    Condition
    Body
    Update


Example:

    int i = 1;          // Initialization

    while (i <= 5)      // Condition
    {
        printf("%d", i); // Body
        i++;             // Update
    }


============================================================
7. INFINITE while LOOP
============================================================

An infinite loop never terminates.

Example:

    while (1)
    {
        printf("Hello");
    }


Because:

    1 → true

forever.


Another example:

    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 5)
    {
        printf("%d", i);
    }


This is also infinite because:

    i

is never updated.


The condition remains:

    1 <= 5

forever.


============================================================
8. HOW TO STOP AN INFINITE LOOP
============================================================

Use:

    break

Example:

    while (1)
    {
        printf("Running");

        if (condition)
        {
            break;
        }
    }


Infinite loops are sometimes intentional in:

    operating systems
    embedded systems
    servers
    event loops
    real-time systems


============================================================
9. do-WHILE LOOP
============================================================

Syntax:

    do
    {
        statements;
    }
    while (condition);


Important:

The body executes at least once.


Flow:

            START
              |
              v
             BODY
              |
              v
          CONDITION
           /     \
        true     false
         |         |
         +-------->EXIT


============================================================
10. SIMPLE do-WHILE
============================================================

    int i = 1;

    do
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }
    while (i <= 5);


Output:

    1 2 3 4 5


============================================================
11. while VS do-WHILE
============================================================

while:

    condition first
    body may execute zero times


do-while:

    body first
    body executes at least once


Example:

    int i = 10;

    while (i < 5)
    {
        printf("Hello");
    }


Output:

    Nothing


Because:

    10 < 5

is false before entering.


Now:

    int i = 10;

    do
    {
        printf("Hello");
    }
    while (i < 5);


Output:

    Hello


Because the body executes first.


============================================================
12. do-WHILE SEMICOLON
============================================================

Important syntax:

    do
    {
        ...
    }
    while (condition);


There is a semicolon after:

    while (condition);


Unlike:

    while (condition)
    {
        ...
    }


This is a common beginner mistake.


============================================================
13. FOR LOOP
============================================================

Syntax:

    for (initialization; condition; update)
    {
        statements;
    }


Example:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Output:

    1 2 3 4 5


============================================================
14. FOR LOOP EXECUTION ORDER
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Execution:

    Initialization
         ↓
    Condition
         ↓
       Body
         ↓
       Update
         ↓
    Condition
         ↓
       Body
         ↓
       Update
         ↓
      ...
         ↓
    Condition false
         ↓
       Exit


Initialization happens only once.


============================================================
15. FOR LOOP COMPONENTS
============================================================

    for (initialization; condition; update)


Initialization:

    int i = 0


Condition:

    i < 10


Update:

    i++


Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        ...
    }


============================================================
16. FOR LOOP WITHOUT INITIALIZATION
============================================================

Valid:

    int i = 0;

    for (; i < 5; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Initialization can be outside the loop.


============================================================
17. FOR LOOP WITHOUT UPDATE
============================================================

Valid:

    int i = 0;

    for (; i < 5;)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }


The update is moved into the body.


============================================================
18. INFINITE FOR LOOP
============================================================

Common syntax:

    for (;;)
    {
        ...
    }


All three expressions are omitted.

It creates an infinite loop.

Equivalent conceptually to:

    while (1)
    {
        ...
    }


============================================================
19. FOR LOOP WITH MULTIPLE VARIABLES
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++, j--)
    {
        printf("%d %d\n", i, j);
    }


This is useful in:

    two-pointer algorithms
    array processing
    searching
    reversing


============================================================
20. WHILE VS FOR
============================================================

Use for when the loop is naturally counter-controlled.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        ...
    }


Use while when termination depends more naturally on
a condition.

Example:

    while (input != 0)
    {
        ...
    }


There is no strict rule; both can often express the same
logic.


============================================================
21. DO-WHILE USE CASE
============================================================

do-while is useful when the operation must happen at least
once.

Classic example:

    menu

    do
    {
        display_menu();
        scanf("%d", &choice);
    }
    while (choice != 4);


The menu should appear at least once.


============================================================
22. COUNTER-CONTROLLED LOOP
============================================================

A counter-controlled loop repeats a known number of times.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        printf("Hello\n");
    }


Iterations:

    10


Common uses:

    arrays
    tables
    repeated calculations
    fixed number of inputs


============================================================
23. SENTINEL-CONTROLLED LOOP
============================================================

A sentinel is a special value used to terminate input.

Example:

    int x;

    scanf("%d", &x);

    while (x != -1)
    {
        printf("You entered %d\n", x);
        scanf("%d", &x);
    }


Here:

    -1

is the sentinel.


============================================================
24. SENTINEL EXAMPLE
============================================================

Program:

    Read numbers until user enters 0.

    int n;

    scanf("%d", &n);

    while (n != 0)
    {
        printf("Number = %d\n", n);
        scanf("%d", &n);
    }


When:

    n = 0

loop terminates.


============================================================
25. INPUT VALIDATION LOOP
============================================================

Example:

    int age;

    do
    {
        printf("Enter age 1-100: ");
        scanf("%d", &age);
    }
    while (age < 1 || age > 100);


The loop continues until valid input is received.


============================================================
26. BREAK
============================================================

break immediately terminates the nearest:

    loop
    switch


Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        if (i == 5)
        {
            break;
        }

        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Output:

    0 1 2 3 4


When:

    i == 5

break executes.


============================================================
27. BREAK IN while
============================================================

    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 10)
    {
        if (i == 6)
        {
            break;
        }

        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }


Output:

    1 2 3 4 5


============================================================
28. CONTINUE
============================================================

continue skips the remaining body of the current iteration.

Example:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    {
        if (i == 3)
        {
            continue;
        }

        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Output:

    1 2 4 5


3 is skipped.


============================================================
29. BREAK VS CONTINUE
============================================================

break:

    Stop the entire loop.


continue:

    Skip current iteration.
    Continue with next iteration.


Example:

    break:

    1 2 3 4
    STOP


    continue:

    1 2 4 5
    LOOP CONTINUES


============================================================
30. CONTINUE IN FOR LOOP
============================================================

Important:

In a for loop, continue transfers control to the
loop-update expression.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        if (i == 2)
            continue;

        printf("%d ", i);
    }


When i == 2:

    continue
       ↓
    i++
       ↓
    condition


So the update still occurs.


============================================================
31. CONTINUE IN WHILE LOOP
============================================================

Be careful.

This can create an infinite loop:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 5)
    {
        if (i == 2)
            continue;

        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }


When i becomes 2:

    continue

executes.

The:

    i++

is skipped.

Therefore:

    i remains 2

forever.


Correct:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 5)
    {
        if (i == 2)
        {
            i++;
            continue;
        }

        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }


Or restructure the loop for clarity.


============================================================
32. NESTED LOOPS
============================================================

A loop inside another loop is called a nested loop.

Example:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++)
        {
            printf("%d %d\n", i, j);
        }
    }


Output:

    1 1
    1 2
    1 3
    2 1
    2 2
    2 3
    3 1
    3 2
    3 3


============================================================
33. HOW NESTED LOOPS WORK
============================================================

Outer loop:

    i = 1

Inner loop runs completely:

    j = 1
    j = 2
    j = 3


Then:

    i = 2

Inner loop starts again:

    j = 1
    j = 2
    j = 3


Then:

    i = 3

Again:

    j = 1
    j = 2
    j = 3


============================================================
34. NESTED LOOP ITERATIONS
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 0; j < 10; j++)
        {
            ...
        }
    }


Outer:

    5 iterations


Inner:

    10 iterations per outer iteration


Total:

    5 × 10 = 50


This leads to time complexity:

    O(n × m)


If both are n:

    O(n²)


============================================================
35. LOOP COMPLEXITY
============================================================

Single loop:

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        ...
    }


Time complexity:

    O(n)


Two nested loops:

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
        {
            ...
        }
    }


Time complexity:

    O(n²)


Three nested loops:

    O(n³)


Understanding this is essential for DSA interviews.


============================================================
36. TWO SEPARATE LOOPS
============================================================

Consider:

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        ...
    }

    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
    {
        ...
    }


Complexity:

    O(n) + O(n)

    = O(2n)

Drop constants:

    O(n)


It is NOT:

    O(n²)


============================================================
37. NESTED LOOP WITH DIFFERENT BOUNDS
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
        {
            ...
        }
    }


Number of iterations:

    0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n-1)

    = n(n-1)/2


Complexity:

    O(n²)


============================================================
38. LOGARITHMIC LOOP
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)
    {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Values:

    1
    2
    4
    8
    16
    32
    ...


Number of iterations:

    O(log n)


This pattern is very important in algorithms.


============================================================
39. DIVIDING LOOP
============================================================

Example:

    for (int n = value; n > 1; n /= 2)
    {
        ...
    }


Complexity:

    O(log n)


This appears in:

    binary search
    divide-and-conquer algorithms
    tree algorithms


============================================================
40. OFF-BY-ONE ERROR
============================================================

An off-by-one error occurs when a loop executes one too many
or one too few times.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++)

Iterations:

    0 through 10

Total:

    11


But:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)

Iterations:

    0 through 9

Total:

    10


Remember:

    < n

usually means:

    n iterations when starting at 0.


============================================================
41. ARRAY INDEXING AND LOOPS
============================================================

C arrays use zero-based indexing.

Example:

    int arr[5];


Valid indexes:

    0
    1
    2
    3
    4


Correct traversal:

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }


Wrong:

    for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }


arr[5] is outside the array.

Accessing it causes undefined behavior.


============================================================
42. ARRAY TRAVERSAL WITH sizeof
============================================================

For an actual array in the same scope:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    size_t n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }


Output:

    10 20 30 40 50


Important:
This technique works for an actual array object, not for an
array parameter that has decayed to a pointer.


============================================================
43. LOOPING THROUGH A STRING
============================================================

C strings end with:

    '\0'


Example:

    char str[] = "Hello";

Memory:

    H e l l o \0


Loop:

    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++)
    {
        printf("%c", str[i]);
    }


Output:

    Hello


============================================================
44. WHILE STRING TRAVERSAL
============================================================

    int i = 0;

    while (str[i] != '\0')
    {
        printf("%c", str[i]);
        i++;
    }


This pattern is fundamental for string programming.


============================================================
45. LOOPING THROUGH A MATRIX
============================================================

Example:

    int matrix[2][3] =
    {
        {1, 2, 3},
        {4, 5, 6}
    };


Traversal:

    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
        {
            printf("%d ", matrix[i][j]);
        }

        printf("\n");
