============================================================
55. REVERSE A NUMBER
============================================================

Example:

    1234

Expected:

    4321


Algorithm:

    reverse = 0

    digit = n % 10

    reverse = reverse * 10 + digit

    n = n / 10


Program:

    int n = 1234;
    int reverse = 0;

    while (n != 0)
    {
        int digit = n % 10;
        reverse = reverse * 10 + digit;
        n /= 10;
    }

    printf("%d\n", reverse);


Output:

    4321


This is an excellent loop practice problem.


============================================================
56. COUNT DIGITS
============================================================

Example:

    12345

Number of digits:

    5


Program:

    int n = 12345;
    int count = 0;

    while (n != 0)
    {
        count++;
        n /= 10;
    }

    printf("%d\n", count);


Output:

    5


Special case:

    n = 0

needs separate handling if you want its digit count to be 1.


============================================================
57. SUM OF DIGITS
============================================================

Example:

    1234

Sum:

    1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10


Program:

    int n = 1234;
    int sum = 0;

    while (n != 0)
    {
        sum += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }

    printf("%d\n", sum);


Output:

    10


============================================================
58. PALINDROME NUMBER
============================================================

Example:

    121

Reverse:

    121

Therefore:

    palindrome


Algorithm:

    Store original number.

    Reverse number using loop.

    Compare:

        original == reverse


Example:

    int n = 121;
    int original = n;
    int reverse = 0;

    while (n != 0)
    {
        int digit = n % 10;
        reverse = reverse * 10 + digit;
        n /= 10;
    }

    if (original == reverse)
        printf("Palindrome");
    else
        printf("Not palindrome");


============================================================
59. PRIME NUMBER USING LOOP
============================================================

A prime number has exactly two positive divisors:

    1
    itself


Basic approach:

    int n = 29;
    int prime = 1;

    if (n < 2)
        prime = 0;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n / i && prime; i++)
    {
        if (n % i == 0)
        {
            prime = 0;
        }
    }

    if (prime)
        printf("Prime");
    else
        printf("Not prime");


Why:

    i <= n / i

instead of:

    i * i <= n

?

It avoids multiplication overflow for sufficiently large
integer values.


