60. BREAK IN SEARCHING
============================================================

Suppose you search for a target:

    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int target = 30;

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] == target)
        {
            printf("Found at index %d\n", i);
            break;
        }
    }


Once found, there is no need to continue searching.


============================================================
61. SENTINEL SEARCH
============================================================

Example:

    Read numbers until -1.

    int n;

    while (1)
    {
        scanf("%d", &n);

        if (n == -1)
            break;

        printf("Processing %d\n", n);
    }


This is an infinite loop with an explicit termination
condition.


============================================================
62. MENU LOOP
============================================================

Example:

    int choice;

    do
    {
        printf("\n1. Add");
        printf("\n2. Delete");
        printf("\n3. Search");
        printf("\n4. Exit");

        printf("\nChoice: ");
        scanf("%d", &choice);

        switch (choice)
        {
            case 1:
                printf("Add");
                break;

            case 2:
                printf("Delete");
                break;

            case 3:
                printf("Search");
                break;

            case 4:
                printf("Exiting");
                break;

            default:
                printf("Invalid choice");
        }

    }
    while (choice != 4);


This combines:

    do-while
    switch
    break
    input
    conditions


============================================================
63. LOOP AND INPUT FAILURE
============================================================

A loop that reads input using scanf must consider whether
scanf succeeded.

Example:

    int x;

    if (scanf("%d", &x) != 1)
    {
        printf("Invalid input");
    }


For robust interactive programs, repeatedly attempting scanf
without handling failed input can cause the same invalid input
to remain unread and lead to an infinite loop.

Input handling is therefore an important part of loop design.


============================================================
64. LOOP VARIABLE SCOPE
============================================================

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        printf("%d", i);
    }


Here:

    i

is scoped to the for statement and its body.

After the loop:

    i

is not available.


If you need it afterward:

    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        ...
    }

    printf("%d", i);


============================================================
65. MODIFYING LOOP COUNTER
============================================================

Avoid unnecessary modifications to the loop counter inside
the body.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        i += 2;
    }


This is valid but makes the number of iterations less obvious.

Prefer clear loop structure when possible.


============================================================
66. COMMON BUG — WRONG CONDITION
============================================================

Wrong:

    for (int i = 0; i > 10; i++)
    {
        printf("%d", i);
    }


Initial condition:

    0 > 10

is false.

So the loop executes zero times.


Correct:

    i < 10


============================================================
67. COMMON BUG — WRONG UPDATE
============================================================

Wrong:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i--)
    {
        ...
    }


i starts at 0.

Condition:

    0 < 10 → true

Then:

    i--

becomes:

    -1

Still:

    -1 < 10 → true

The loop continues forever.

Correct:

    i++


============================================================
68. COMMON BUG — OFF-BY-ONE
============================================================

Suppose an array has 10 elements.

Valid indexes:

    0 through 9


Correct:

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)


Wrong:

    for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++)


Because i becomes 10.


============================================================
69. COMMON BUG — MODIFYING WRONG VARIABLE
============================================================

Wrong:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 10)
    {
        printf("%d", i);
        int j = 0;
        j++;
    }


i never changes.

Therefore the loop is infinite.


Correct:

    i++;


============================================================
70. COMMON BUG — CONTINUE IN WHILE
============================================================

Dangerous:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 10)
    {
        if (i == 5)
            continue;

        i++;
    }


When i == 5:

    continue

executes forever.

Always ensure the loop's progress mechanism still happens
when continue is reached.


============================================================
71. COMMON BUG — FLOAT LOOP COUNTER
============================================================

Avoid using floating-point variables as loop counters when
exact iteration counts matter.

Example:

    for (double x = 0.0; x <= 1.0; x += 0.1)
    {
        ...
    }


Binary floating-point cannot represent many decimal fractions
exactly, so the number of iterations may not behave exactly
as expected.

Prefer integer counters:

    for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++)
    {
        double x = i / 10.0;
        ...
    }


============================================================
72. LOOP WITH MULTIPLE CONDITIONS
============================================================

Example:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 100 && !found)
    {
        ...
        i++;
    }


The loop continues while:

    i < 100

AND:

    found is false.


Short-circuit evaluation applies to &&.


============================================================
73. LOOP AND SHORT-CIRCUIT
============================================================

Example:

    while (ptr != NULL && *ptr != '\0')
    {
        ...
        ptr++;
    }


First:

    ptr != NULL


If false:

    *ptr

is not evaluated.


This is a very common safe pattern.


============================================================
74. LOOP INVARIANT — INTRODUCTION
============================================================

A loop invariant is a property that remains true before and
after each iteration.

Example:

    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        sum += arr[i];
    }


A useful invariant:

    sum contains the sum of elements processed so far.


Loop invariants are important for:

    algorithm correctness
    DSA
    formal reasoning
    interviews


============================================================
75. LOOP TERMINATION
============================================================

A well-designed loop should have:

    initialization
    progress
    termination condition


Example:

    int i = 0;

    while (i < 10)
    {
        ...
        i++;
    }


Termination reasoning:

    i increases every iteration.

Eventually:

    i == 10

Then:

    i < 10

becomes false.


============================================================
76. LOOP COMPLEXITY CHEAT SHEET
============================================================

Pattern:

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)

Complexity:

    O(n)


Pattern:

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (j = 0; j < n; j++)

Complexity:

    O(n²)


Pattern:

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (j = 0; j < m; j++)

Complexity:

    O(nm)


Pattern:

    for (i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)

Complexity:

    O(log n)


Pattern:

    for (i = n; i > 0; i /= 2)

Complexity:

    O(log n)


Pattern:

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (j = i; j < n; j++)

Complexity:

    O(n²)


============================================================
77. LOOP OPTIMIZATION MINDSET
============================================================

Do not optimize loops blindly.

First ask:

    Is the algorithm correct?
    What is its complexity?
    Is the loop necessary?
    Can work be moved outside?
    Can the algorithm be improved?


Example:

    O(n²)

may sometimes be improved to:

    O(n log n)

or:

    O(n)


Algorithm selection usually matters more than tiny syntax
optimizations.


============================================================
78. BREAK VS RETURN
============================================================

break:

    exits the nearest loop/switch.


return:

    exits the entire current function.


Example:

    int find(void)
    {
        for (...)
        {
            if (...)
            {
                return 1;
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }


return exits the function completely.


============================================================
79. GOTO VS LOOPS
============================================================

C also has:

    goto


But normal repetitive logic should usually use:

    for
    while
    do-while


Avoid unnecessary goto because it can make control flow
harder to understand.

There are specialized low-level situations where goto can be
appropriate, particularly centralized cleanup in C.


